      "text": "To find the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\), we first need to determine the repeating decimal of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\).\n\n### Step 1: Find the decimal expansion of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\)\n\nFirst, perform the division \\(5 \\div 14\\):\n\n\\[\n5 \\div 14 = 0.357142857142\\ldots\n\\]\n\nThe decimal expansion of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\) is \\(0.\\overline{357142}\\), which means the digits \"357142\" repeat indefinitely.\n\n### Step 2: Identify the repeating cycle\n\nThe repeating cycle in the decimal expansion of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\) is \"357142\". This cycle has a length of 6 digits:\n\n\\[\n357142, 357142, \\ldots\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the position of the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit within this repeating cycle. To do this, we calculate the remainder when 1314 is divided by 6:\n\n\\[\n1314 \\div 6 = 219 \\text{ remainder } 0\n\\]\n\nA remainder of 0 indicates that the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit corresponds to the last digit of the repeating cycle. The repeating cycle \"357142\" has 6 digits, and the last digit is \"2\".\n\n### Step 3: Conclusion\n\nThe \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
